  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiescent weather expected through the end of \nthe week.\n\nHigh pressure moves offshore today turning the winds out of the\nsouth. This will yield a milder day with temps climbing back \ninto the 70s. Temps warm up to near 80 Thu before the passage \nof another dry cold front. Temps cool off slightly for the end \nof the week as weak high pressure of Canadian origin builds in. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increased rain potential late this weekend into\nearly next week. \n\nThe main feature to watch in the extended portion of the forecast \nwill be what becomes of Tropical Depression 9. Key steering features \nwill be a ridge in the western Atlantic, a trough/closed low over \nTexas, and eventually a trough ejecting from the northern Rockies. \nThe NHC forecast has the remnants of this system moving into the \nTennessee and Ohio Valleys over the weekend, although there still \nremains considerable uncertainty on the track and especially forward \nspeed of the system. Even despite this westward position of the \ncyclone, moisture from both the Gulf of America and Atlantic \nwill likely be transported northward into the Mid Atlantic. Rain\nwill become increasingly likely Sun, although as mentioned, \nthis timing could vary a bit. The remnant low could then move \neastward, potentially extending rain chances into Monday. At \nthis juncture, the rain will be the main impact from this \nsystem, but we will continue to monitor any changing trends.\n\nIt will be important to stay tuned to the forecast over the next few \ndays, and monitor hurricanes.gov for updated details on Tropical \nDepression 9.\n\nDaytime temperatures will likely be seasonal through the weekend and \nearly next week but will take a dip when rain is the most \nwidespread. Meanwhile overnight lows will be mild thanks to \nincreasing clouds and dew points.",
